        "content": "To find the least positive integer $x$ satisfying the system of congruences:\n1) $x \\equiv 17 \\pmod{97}$\n2) $x \\equiv 31 \\pmod{101}$\n3) $x \\equiv 43 \\pmod{103}$\n\nSince 97, 101, and 103 are all prime numbers, they are pairwise coprime. We can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem.\n\n**Step 1: Solve the first two congruences.**\n$x = 97k + 17$. Substituting into the second congruence:\n$97k + 17 \\equiv 31 \\pmod{101}$\n$-4k \\equiv 14 \\pmod{101}$\nDividing by -2 (since $\\gcd(-2, 101) = 1$):\n$2k \\equiv -7 \\equiv 94 \\pmod{101}$\n$k \\equiv 47 \\pmod{101}$\nSo, $x = 97(47) + 17 = 4559 + 17 = 4576$.\nThe general solution for the first two is $x \\equiv 4576 \\pmod{97 \\times 101}$, where $97 \\times 101 = 9797$.\n\n**Step 2: Solve for the third congruence.**\n$x = 9797m + 4576$. Substituting into the third congruence:\n$9797m + 4576 \\equiv 43 \\pmod{103}$\nFirst, reduce the coefficients modulo 103:\n$9797 = 103 \\times 95 + 12 \\implies 9797 \\equiv 12 \\pmod{103}$\n$4576 = 103 \\times 44 + 44 \\implies 4576 \\equiv 44 \\pmod{103}$\nThe equation becomes:\n$12m + 44 \\equiv 43 \\pmod{103}$\n$12m \\equiv -1 \\equiv 102 \\pmod{103}$\nDivide by 6 (since $\\gcd(6, 103) = 1$):\n$2m \\equiv 17 \\equiv 120 \\pmod{103}$\n$m \\equiv 60 \\pmod{103}$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the final value of x.**\n$x = 9797(60) + 4576$\n$x = 587820 + 4576$\n$x = 592396$\n\n**Verification:**\n$592396 = 97 \\times 6107 + 17 \\checkmark$\n$592396 = 101 \\times 5865 + 31 \\checkmark$\n$592396 = 103 \\times 5751 + 43 \\checkmark$\n\nThe least positive integer is 592396.",
